{
  "_comment": "Every 2026 Truth-in-Taxation notice in Utah — not just Eagle Mountain's, and not just cities. One row per notice, exactly as the Utah Taxpayers Association compiled them from the counties' own notices: certified rate, proposed rate, the increase measured against the certified rate, the dollar effect on that entity's average home, the revenue the increase raises, and when and where the hearing is. Rates are decimal multipliers on taxable value. Nothing here is derived except the `adopted` block, which carries Eagle Mountain's August 6 vote through the same notice — see its own `derivation`.",
  "year": 2026,
  "source": {
    "title": "2026 Truth-in-Taxation list",
    "publisher": "Utah Taxpayers Association",
    "url": "https://utahtaxpayers.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/2026-Truth-in-Taxation-list-Sheet1-2.pdf",
    "retrieved": "2026-08-07",
    "reported_by": {
      "title": "67 Utah taxing entities seeking $93.58M in tax hikes between them, taxpayers group says",
      "publisher": "KSL.com",
      "url": "https://www.ksl.com/article/51606015/67-utah-taxing-entities-seeking-9358m-in-tax-hikes-between-them-taxpayers-group-says"
    }
  },
  "totals": {
    "notices": 79,
    "entities": 68,
    "counties": 18,
    "revenue": 96055660,
    "revenue_headline": 93580522,
    "hearings_from": "2026-08-03",
    "hearings_to": "2026-08-27"
  },
  "eagle_mountain": {
    "entity": "Eagle Mountain City",
    "adopted": {
      "rate": 0.0015,
      "rate_display": "0.001500",
      "pct": 183.02,
      "annual_increase": 260.35,
      "revenue": 5591594,
      "budget_total": 8646795,
      "home_value": 488000,
      "taxable_value": 268400,
      "date": "2026-08-06",
      "meeting_id": 747,
      "vote": "Passed 5–0 on a roll call — moved by Whiting, seconded by Wright; Wood, Whiting, Wright, Clark and Huish all in favour",
      "motion": "Approves and adopts a property tax rate of 0.001500 for fiscal year 2026 to 2027. This rate exceeds the certified tax rate of 0.000530 and will result in an increase in the city's ad valorem property tax budgeted revenue of approximately $5,591,594, or 183.02% above the city's property tax budgeted revenue for the prior year, exclusive of new growth.",
      "source": {
        "title": "City Council meeting, August 6, 2026 — Truth-in-Taxation hearing, item 4.A",
        "publisher": "Eagle Mountain City",
        "url": "meetings.html?id=747",
        "retrieved": "2026-08-07"
      },
      "derivation": "The rate, the 183.02% and the $5,591,594 are read verbatim from the motion Councilmember Whiting made and the council passed 5–0; nothing about them is inferred. The $260.35 is the one derived figure: the Truth-in-Taxation notice's own $268,400 taxable value — 55% of a $488,000 home — times the 0.000970 the rate rose, which is the same arithmetic behind the $314.03 on the notice and behind every other entity's dollar column. Around the table the council put it as “about $250 a year” and “closer to 70 cents per day”; those are the same figure spoken loosely, not a different one."
    }
  },
  "caveats": [
    "Every percentage here is the increase over that entity's own certified rate — the rate that would raise the same money as last year. It is not an increase in anyone's total tax bill: a city, a school district, a water district and sometimes a fire district all levy on the same parcel, and only one of them is on this list.",
    "The dollar figure is the effect on an average home in that entity, and each entity picked its own average. They run from $300,000 in Boulder to $1,895,000 in Summit County's slice of the Weber Basin district, so the dollar column compares typical households, not identical ones.",
    "79 notices, 68 entities: Central Utah Water Conservancy District notices in seven counties, Weber Basin in five, and Draper in two, each with its own hearing and its own figures. Ranking by revenue counts each notice separately, so those entities' totals are split across rows.",
    "The Utah Taxpayers Association's own headline — 67 entities, $93,580,522 — leaves out Ogden Valley City, which incorporated too recently to have a certified rate and shows ‘Not Available’ in both rate columns. Its $2,475,138 is included in the $96.1M total here, and it is ranked on the 512.61% its notice states.",
    "These are proposals. A hearing can end in a smaller increase than the one noticed, or in none at all — Eagle Mountain's did: the council noticed 0.001700 and adopted 0.001500 on August 6, after nearly three hours of public comment. Every other row is still the noticed figure."
  ],
